1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,120
The scanner read the label. The system confirmed the route.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,600
Every step worked, except the package went to the wrong

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:12,240
address. When the information is wrong, confirmation alone cannot make

4
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:12,740
it correct.

5
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:25,280
A conversation can feel successful even when the information is

6
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:28,580
wrong. That is the question behind our project: when collecting

7
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:32,660
structured information, when does chat help, and when is a

8
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:37,155
traditional form a better choice? We are Team C-10, teamGARI:

9
00:00:37,155 --> 00:00:42,967
Raja Suleman, Raj Lakhani, Awais Basheer, and Muhammad Immad.

10
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:46,500
Our task is setting up a study-planner profile with courses,

11
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:50,560
exam dates, and work evenings. We compare the two interfaces

12
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:54,480
through three questions: how long does the interaction take, what

13
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:58,820
information gets confirmed, and how easily can the user check

14
00:00:58,820 --> 00:01:00,060
and correct it?

15
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:05,580
Before the demonstration, one important distinction: our chat is a

16
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:08,060
scripted simulation, not a live language model.

17
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:12,660
Its mistakes were deliberately programmed to illustrate possible failure modes.

18
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:16,290
Our results come from one team member’s run, so they

19
00:01:16,290 --> 00:01:19,680
are illustrative, not a validated comparison.

20
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:24,400
Here are the two versions of our prototype: the form

21
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,440
on the left and the scripted chat on the right.

22
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:29,880
In the first scenario, the student has two exams next

23
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:35,180
week, for CS3014 and MT1003, and works Tuesday and Thursday

24
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:39,740
evenings. In chat, the message produces a summary showing the

25
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:42,700
courses, the exam week, and the work evenings.

26
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,880
The tester confirms it, and the displayed timer stops at

27
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,860
8.6 seconds. On the form, the tester selects the courses,

28
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:53,520
enters individual exam dates, and chooses the work evenings.

29
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:56,800
The displayed timer stops at 12.5 seconds.

30
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:00,760
Both complete this scenario without the planted mistakes.

31
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,900
However, the outputs differ in detail: chat records the week,

32
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:06,920
while the form records exact dates.

33
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,060
Keep that difference in mind when comparing them.

34
00:02:10,227 --> 00:02:13,426
Now the input is less precise: “Exams are after the

35
00:02:13,426 --> 00:02:16,886
break, I’m in CS 3041, and I work Mondays.” There

36
00:02:16,886 --> 00:02:19,907
is no exact exam date. The course code is also

37
00:02:19,907 --> 00:02:22,946
mistyped: the intended code is CS3014.

38
00:02:23,407 --> 00:02:26,407
Watch the chat summary. It supplies the twenty-second of November,

39
00:02:26,907 --> 00:02:29,527
even though the message never gave that date.

40
00:02:30,166 --> 00:02:32,807
It also carries the incorrect course code into the summary.

41
00:02:33,287 --> 00:02:36,227
The tester highlights the date, but still selects Confirm.

42
00:02:36,926 --> 00:02:40,666
The interface displays “Profile saved,” with both incorrect values accepted.

43
00:02:41,326 --> 00:02:43,826
This is a programmed demonstration of the failure, not an

44
00:02:43,826 --> 00:02:49,727
observed language-model prediction. The design question it illustrates is still

45
00:02:49,727 --> 00:02:53,347
useful: does a confirmation button help if the user misses

46
00:02:53,347 --> 00:02:54,606
what needs correcting?

47
00:02:55,153 --> 00:02:59,153
On the form, the tester leaves the required exam date

48
00:02:59,373 --> 00:03:02,333
and work evenings blank, then tries to save.

49
00:03:02,573 --> 00:03:04,933
An error summary identifies the two missing entries.

50
00:03:05,573 --> 00:03:08,613
The tester supplies the date, selects Monday, and saves successfully.

51
00:03:09,353 --> 00:03:11,813
The displayed timer stops at 18.7 seconds.

52
00:03:12,273 --> 00:03:15,953
The form catches missing required information in this run.

53
00:03:15,953 --> 00:03:18,453
That does not mean it can detect every wrong answer.

54
00:03:18,813 --> 00:03:22,033
The contrast is that this form demands an explicit value,

55
00:03:21,986 --> 00:03:25,473
while our scripted chat substitutes a date and accepts the

56
00:03:25,473 --> 00:03:25,960
typo.

57
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:30,700
What do these results actually tell us?

58
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:35,280
First, time. The displayed chat times were 8.6 seconds in

59
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:38,740
scenario one and 13 seconds in scenario two.

60
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:42,180
The form times were 12.5 and 18.7 seconds.

61
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:45,460
However, the timers start at different points.

62
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:49,760
The form starts timing at the first input; chat starts

63
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:52,940
when the message is sent, excluding message preparation.

64
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:56,800
The interfaces also collect different levels of date detail.

65
00:03:57,420 --> 00:04:01,640
These are demonstration timings, not a controlled test proving that

66
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:06,820
chat is faster. Second, errors. In the messy scenario, the

67
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:10,760
chat accepts two incorrect values, while the form blocks missing

68
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:15,700
entries. Third, control. A summary gives users an opportunity to

69
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:19,580
review, but simply showing information does not guarantee that mistakes

70
00:04:19,580 --> 00:04:20,180
will be noticed.

71
00:04:23,226 --> 00:04:25,306
Our slides also bring in published research.

72
00:04:25,886 --> 00:04:28,566
The studies we cite report that a single-page form was

73
00:04:28,566 --> 00:04:32,066
fastest in one comparison, and that a chatbot took longer

74
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:35,426
in another. The cited work by Wei and colleagues examines

75
00:04:35,426 --> 00:04:39,626
how well a language-model chatbot collects target information, including the

76
00:04:39,626 --> 00:04:42,926
risk of leaving information out. That is a different measure

77
00:04:42,926 --> 00:04:46,846
from simply counting wrong values. These studies use different tasks

78
00:04:46,846 --> 00:04:49,526
and systems, so their results should not be treated as

79
00:04:49,696 --> 00:04:54,306
one universal ranking. Our demonstration illustrates a design problem.

80
00:04:54,646 --> 00:04:57,566
It does not establish how often real chatbots fail, or

81
00:04:57,566 --> 00:04:59,526
overturn findings from larger studies.

82
00:04:59,812 --> 00:05:02,532
There are important limits to our work.

83
00:05:02,712 --> 00:05:06,152
We built the prototype ourselves, tested it with one team

84
00:05:06,152 --> 00:05:09,112
member, and deliberately scripted the chat’s mistakes.

85
00:05:09,812 --> 00:05:13,592
We cannot generalise these results to all users or to

86
00:05:13,592 --> 00:05:18,472
real language models. Our keyboard-only pass also found problems in

87
00:05:18,472 --> 00:05:24,012
both interfaces. In chat, reaching Confirm required moving through other

88
00:05:24,012 --> 00:05:27,552
controls because focus was not moved to the new summary.

89
00:05:27,552 --> 00:05:32,072
In the form, we encountered difficulty dismissing the browser’s date

90
00:05:32,072 --> 00:05:35,732
picker with the keyboard. That behaviour may depend on the

91
00:05:35,732 --> 00:05:41,132
browser and platform. These observations show why accessibility needs its

92
00:05:41,132 --> 00:05:45,232
own evaluation. A visually clear interface is not enough.

93
00:05:45,479 --> 00:05:47,939
We did not test with a screen reader.

94
00:05:47,939 --> 00:05:51,979
The prototype includes a conversation log role, but that alone

95
00:05:51,979 --> 00:05:55,079
does not verify that new replies are announced correctly.

96
00:05:55,419 --> 00:05:58,619
We would need to test announcements, focus order, and correction

97
00:05:58,619 --> 00:06:03,699
controls with assistive technology. We also distinguish research evidence from

98
00:06:03,699 --> 00:06:08,179
marketing. The Typeform completion claim discussed in our slides is

99
00:06:08,169 --> 00:06:12,579
a secondhand vendor claim about conversational forms, not evidence that

100
00:06:12,626 --> 00:06:16,039
language-model chat is better. We did not test it.

101
00:06:16,459 --> 00:06:20,019
Finally, free text can invite people to share more information

102
00:06:20,019 --> 00:06:23,759
than necessary. If a future version sends messages to an

103
00:06:23,759 --> 00:06:27,539
external service, that creates additional privacy considerations.

104
00:06:28,279 --> 00:06:34,266
Our demonstration uses fake data and a local scripted parser.

105
00:06:35,246 --> 00:06:38,746
Our recommendation is to start with the information the task

106
00:06:38,746 --> 00:06:42,686
requires. When the fields are known and correctness matters, a

107
00:06:42,686 --> 00:06:46,525
form makes those requirements visible. Chat can help people express

108
00:06:46,535 --> 00:06:50,645
messy or narrative information, but uncertainty needs to stay visible,

109
00:06:50,645 --> 00:06:52,725
and corrections need to be easy.

110
00:06:53,366 --> 00:06:57,266
For this task, we propose a hybrid: let free text

111
00:06:57,275 --> 00:07:00,525
pre-fill a form, then let the user inspect and edit

112
00:07:00,525 --> 00:07:03,766
the fields before confirming. Ask for missing dates instead of

113
00:07:03,766 --> 00:07:07,826
guessing them, and flag an unrecognised course code.

114
00:07:08,285 --> 00:07:11,045
This is our proposed improvement, not a feature we tested.

115
00:07:11,045 --> 00:07:15,085
For our project, this informs the D3 and D4 input-flow

116
00:07:15,085 --> 00:07:17,345
design and the D5 accessibility evaluation.

117
00:07:18,045 --> 00:07:21,225
Next, we would test with real users, use consistent timing

118
00:07:21,225 --> 00:07:25,345
rules, check screen-reader behaviour, and measure errors across repeated runs.

119
00:07:25,845 --> 00:07:28,366
The key question is not just whether the interaction feels

120
00:07:28,366 --> 00:07:32,425
natural. It is whether people can understand and control what

121
00:07:32,425 --> 00:07:34,425
the system records. Thank you for watching.
